<div type="letter">
<div type="mainBody">
<p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: The address is: <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <name type="geo" key="ID_MISSING">Gebers Forlag, Stockholm</name>
                        </seg>. Will you please send a copy also to: <name type="person" key="nutt90">
                            Mr Myron Nutting</name>,<name type="geo" key="ID_MISSING"> 9 rue Falgrière</name>, <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name>? This is the last I shall trouble you to send. After hours of search I cannot find <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">the typed plan</seg> of <name type="work" key="W_USLYSSES">
                            <hi rend="underline">Ulysses</hi>
                        </name>. It must be in one of my many cases nailed up somewhere. Could you have a copy of it made for me and forwarded here? I ask because an admirer of the book wants to do an <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">article</seg> for next week’s <hi rend="underline">Sunday</hi> <hi rend="underline">Times</hi> and as usual I have to explain my explanations. If you have no typist available in your office don’t trouble.</p>
<closer>
<salute>With kind regards</salute>
                    <salute>	sincerely yours</salute>
                               	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
</closer>

</div>
</div>

<div type="notes">

<note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">Hugo Gebers Förlag was a Swedish publishing house founded in 1887. They published the first Swedish edition of <hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work" key="W_PORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>
                    </hi> in 1921. </note>

<note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">JJ had two slightly different plans (or schemata) for <hi rend="italic">Ulysses</hi>, one of which he sent to the Italian critic Carlo Linati and a later version to his friend Stuart Gilbert.</note>

<note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">No article about <hi rend="italic">Ulysses</hi> appeared in the <hi rend="italic">Sunday Times</hi> of 1922.</note>
</div>

